Certainly! The GCM (Galois/Counter Mode) algorithm is a type of Authenticated Encryption with Associated Data (AEAD) construction that is widely used for securing communication channels. The AAD (Authentication and Deduplication) used in GCM, allows encrypting and authenticating a message in a single pass, reducing computational overhead.

Now, to answer your specific question, to use the `updateAAD` method in the GCM AEAD cipher class, you would pass in the Authentication and Deduplication value, usually as a byte array, as an argument. The method will update the AAD block in the GCM state.

Here's an example in Java using the Bouncy Castle library:
```java
import org.bouncycastle.crypto.BufferedBlockCipher;
import org.bouncycastle.crypto.paddings.GCMBlockCipherPadding;
import org.bouncycastle.crypto.params.GCMParameters;

public class GCMExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        byte[] nonce = "authentication_nonce".getBytes(); // a byte array containing your authentication nonce
        byte[] ad = "deduplication_data".getBytes(); // a byte array containing your deduplication data
        byte[] message = "hello world".getBytes(); // the message you want to encrypt

        // create a GCM AEAD cipher instance with your key and nonce
        GCMParameters params = new GCMParameters(new SecretKeySpec(key.getBytes(), "AES"), nonce);
        BufferedBlockCipher cipher = new BufferedBlockCipher(new AEADWrapper(params));

        // initialize the cipher with your AAD
        cipher.init(false, new GCMParameters(new SecretKeySpec(key.getBytes(), "AES"), ad));

        // update the AAD by adding the message's length and the message itself
        cipher.updateAAD(message);

        // perform the encryption and authentication
        byte[] encrypted = cipher.doFinal(message);

        // print the encrypted message and AAD
        System.out.println("Encrypted message with AAD: " + new String(encrypted));
    }
}
